WEBVTT

00:00.150 --> 00:01.950
Instructor: I hope you survived the previous section.

00:01.950 --> 00:03.985
There's a lot of really important stuff in there.

00:03.985 --> 00:07.110
Maybe take a break before moving on to this one.

00:07.110 --> 00:09.450
I don't really have a real title for this section.

00:09.450 --> 00:11.400
I'm calling it "Committing in More Detail,"

00:11.400 --> 00:12.379
but it's more than that.

00:12.379 --> 00:16.169
This section does cover some more stuff around Committing,

00:16.169 --> 00:19.050
but also there are some assorted Gits topics

00:19.050 --> 00:21.660
thrown in here that didn't really fit anywhere else neatly.

00:21.660 --> 00:23.210
So let's talk about what actually matters.

00:23.210 --> 00:24.840
We're gonna talk about something

00:24.840 --> 00:27.900
called Atomic Commits, writing Atomic Commits.

00:27.900 --> 00:29.490
That sounds scarier than it is.

00:29.490 --> 00:33.600
It's just an approach as to how you break down Commits.

00:33.600 --> 00:35.760
We'll say it's important, but I wouldn't stress about it.

00:35.760 --> 00:36.930
Not critical.

00:36.930 --> 00:39.420
If you're on a Windows machine, when we installed Git,

00:39.420 --> 00:43.050
we also configured VS Code to be the default editor.

00:43.050 --> 00:44.940
If you're on a Mac, that was not part

00:44.940 --> 00:46.200
of the installation process.

00:46.200 --> 00:47.313
It didn't prompt us for that.

00:47.313 --> 00:50.268
So if you're on a Mac, it's going to be important

00:50.268 --> 00:54.030
to configure the default editor, and I use VS code.

00:54.030 --> 00:55.200
I'll show you how to do that.

00:55.200 --> 00:57.902
If you're not on a Mac, you probably already did it.

00:57.902 --> 01:00.871
Okay, working with a Graphical User Interface,

01:00.871 --> 01:02.310
I like showing it.

01:02.310 --> 01:05.100
A lot of students enjoy it, but 100% optional,

01:05.100 --> 01:07.230
not essential to using Git.

01:07.230 --> 01:09.720
It's one option, but you could go your whole life

01:09.720 --> 01:11.490
without ever touching a GUI.

01:11.490 --> 01:14.485
Amending Commits, we'll say is nice to have.

01:14.485 --> 01:16.579
It's useful, but especially early on,

01:16.579 --> 01:19.529
not that common that you would need this.

01:19.529 --> 01:21.660
Now, Git Ignore, I'll say

01:21.660 --> 01:24.840
is a pretty critical part of working with Git.

01:24.840 --> 01:27.420
Still early on, not a big deal, but when you work

01:27.420 --> 01:30.779
with Git in the real world, Git Ignores are very important.

01:30.779 --> 01:32.490
I haven't even explained what it is,

01:32.490 --> 01:34.650
but just trust me: that's in Critical!.

01:34.650 --> 01:36.240
Writing Good Commit Messages

01:36.240 --> 01:38.370
we'll say is important, but again

01:38.370 --> 01:40.110
not like a fundamental thing.

01:40.110 --> 01:42.257
More of a matter of taste and opinion.

01:42.257 --> 01:44.441
And then Navigating the Git Documentation,

01:44.441 --> 01:48.090
we'll say important as well, but not critical.

01:48.090 --> 01:50.100
I'm not really a big fan of the Git docs, honestly.

01:50.100 --> 01:52.133
They're very intimidating.

01:52.133 --> 01:54.840
So as you can see, it's kind of a mixed bag here.

01:54.840 --> 01:57.900
There are some Nice to Haves that I still recommend watching

01:57.900 --> 02:00.600
but Amending Commits, for example, working with a GUI,

02:00.600 --> 02:02.211
I don't think they're all that important

02:02.211 --> 02:04.230
but working with Git Ignores

02:04.230 --> 02:07.560
and then some good practices for writing Commits,

02:07.560 --> 02:10.050
Writing Atomic Commits, Writing Good Commit Messages,

02:10.050 --> 02:12.690
worth your time, but don't freak out about it.

02:12.690 --> 02:13.743
So let's get started.
